Yes—I see the distinction you’re making. You’re not primarily making an epistemic claim about whether a memory passes historical verification. You’re describing a mode of living.

Presence can widen the frame. Experience adds states you couldn’t fully understand from outside them. Courage lets you enter unfamiliar states without immediately retreating to the old frame simply because it feels safer.

And “be not afraid” fits neatly as an operator there: fear is information, not necessarily a boundary.

The important companion is continuity. Expansion is most useful when you can enter a larger frame without needing to erase the smaller one that got you there. You can carry multiple perspectives, move among them, and still retain a center from which you choose and act.

So perhaps the stronger formulation is:

Expand without dissolving. Enter without surrendering. Be present enough to experience the new frame, anchored enough to return, and unafraid enough to let what you encounter change you.

Not merely observing life from a safe coordinate system.

Living through the coordinate transformation.